{
  "title": "Combination Sum II",
  "description": "Given a list of positive integers `candidates`, which may contain repeated values, and a positive integer `target`, find every combination of candidates that adds up to `target`. Each **position** in `candidates` may be used **at most once**.\n\nReturn each combination with its values in ascending order, and list each distinct combination of values once. Return the combinations in ascending lexicographic order.\n\n### Constraints\n\n- `1 <= candidates.size() <= 16`\n- `1 <= candidates[i] <= 50`\n- `1 <= target <= 30`\n- Inputs are passed as function arguments and the answer is returned from the function. Do not read from standard input or print the result.\n",
  "reference": "void pick(const vector<int>& candidates, size_t start, int remaining, vector<int>& chosen, vector<vector<int>>& out) {\n    if (remaining == 0) {\n        out.push_back(chosen);\n        return;\n    }\n    for (size_t i = start; i < candidates.size() && candidates[i] <= remaining; ++i) {\n        if (i > start && candidates[i] == candidates[i - 1]) {\n            continue;\n        }\n        chosen.push_back(candidates[i]);\n        pick(candidates, i + 1, remaining - candidates[i], chosen, out);\n        chosen.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<vector<int>> combination_sum_once(vector<int> candidates, int target) {\n    sort(candidates.begin(), candidates.end());\n    vector<vector<int>> out;\n    vector<int> chosen;\n    pick(candidates, 0, target, chosen, out);\n    return out;\n}\n",
  "template": "vector<vector<int>> combination_sum_once(vector<int> candidates, int target) {\n    // Return every distinct combination that sums to target, using each position once.\n    return {};\n}\n",
  "tags": [
    "Arrays",
    "Backtracking",
    "Sorting"
  ],
  "examples": [
    {
      "test_number": 0,
      "explanation": "Four different combinations reach 8. The list has two 1s, so 1 + 1 + 6 is allowed, but 1 + 7 is listed once even though either 1 could be used."
    },
    {
      "test_number": 1,
      "explanation": "5 can be made as 1 + 2 + 2, using two of the three 2s, or as the single value 5."
    }
  ],
